\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces What the pipeline recovers, for the best-calibrated fine-channel configuration, against injected amplitude and drift fraction $f_{\rm  drift}$ (injected drift as a fraction of the searched ceiling). Carriers with $f_{\rm  drift}\leq 0.25$ are absorbed entirely by the time-median subtraction at every amplitude; those with $f_{\rm  drift}\geq 0.5$ recover progressively, from 42--83{} per cent between $5\sigma $ and $10\sigma $. Trial-level records were not retained, so surviving fractions pool grid phase, sub-channel placement and the three drifting rows: the figure is drawn as discrete cells, not an interpolated surface, and each column's hatched rows share one pooled measurement ($n=24$ per amplitude) rather than fifteen independent ones. Intervals are binomial Wilson 68 per cent; absorbed rows are measured zeros.}}{8}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:compsurface}{{4}{8}{What the pipeline recovers, for the best-calibrated fine-channel configuration, against injected amplitude and drift fraction $f_{\rm drift}$ (injected drift as a fraction of the searched ceiling). Carriers with $f_{\rm drift}\leq 0.25$ are absorbed entirely by the time-median subtraction at every amplitude; those with $f_{\rm drift}\geq 0.5$ recover progressively, from \RecThresh --\RecTwice {} per cent between $5\sigma $ and $10\sigma $. Trial-level records were not retained, so surviving fractions pool grid phase, sub-channel placement and the three drifting rows: the figure is drawn as discrete cells, not an interpolated surface, and each column's hatched rows share one pooled measurement ($n=24$ per amplitude) rather than fifteen independent ones. Intervals are binomial Wilson 68 per cent; absorbed rows are measured zeros}{figure.4}{}}
